.Dd September 24, 2026
.Dt RMUSER 8
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm rmuser
.Nd remove a user account
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl yvh
.Op Fl -keep-home
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Op Ar name ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
removes a user account from the DirectoryServices property lists, along with
everything that belonged to it: its group memberships, its crontab, its queued
.Xr at 1
jobs, its processes and, unless told otherwise, its home directory.
.Pp
System accounts live in
.Pa /etc/master.passwd
and are removed with
.Xr pw 8 .
.Nm
refuses them, and refuses root.
.Pp
Each named account is shown and confirmed before anything happens:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# rmuser joe
Matching password entry:

joe:*:5001:5001:Joe Maloney:/Local/Users/joe:/bin/zsh

Is this the entry you wish to remove? (yes/no) [yes]:
Remove user's home directory (/Local/Users/joe)? (yes/no) [yes]:
Removing user (joe): home passwd.
.Ed
.Pp
Both questions default to yes, which is what someone removing an account
usually means, while still leaving room to object.
.Fl -keep-home
answers the second one no without asking, and
.Fl y
answers both yes.
.Ss Order of operations
The record is removed before the home directory, deliberately.
If the write fails the account still exists, and an account whose files have
already been deleted is a worse position to be in than one with a home nobody
is using.
.Ss Live sessions
A user with an open login session is reported before their processes are
killed, because a
.Dv SIGKILL
arriving in somebody's shell without warning tends to get blamed on the
machine.
The count is printed whether or not
.Fl v
was given.
.Ss The private group
.Xr adduser 8
gives each account a group of its own: same name, same gid, no other
members.
.Nm
removes that group along with the account.
A group is only treated as private when all three hold \(em it shares the
name, it shares the gid, and nothing else is left in it.
A group that merely shares the name, or that still has members, belongs to
somebody and is left alone.
.Pp
Without this every removed account would leave an orphan group behind, and
the next account allocated that gid would inherit a group named after
somebody else.
.Ss The last administrator
.Nm
refuses to remove the only remaining member of the
.Li admin
group unless
.Fl y
is given.
Root ships with no password, so
.Li admin
is the only way to reach
.Xr sudo 8 ;
removing the last one leaves single-user mode as the only way back in.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--keep-home"
.It Fl -keep-home
Leave the home directory alone.
.It Fl f Ar file
Read account names from
.Ar file ,
one per line, or from standard input when it is
.Fl .
Blank lines and lines beginning with
.Ql #
are ignored.
Nothing is confirmed, as with
.Fl y .
A name that fails is reported and the rest of the file is still processed.
.It Fl h
Print a usage summary and exit.
.It Fl v
Say what is being removed as each step happens.
.It Fl y
Answer yes to every question.
.El
.Sh WHAT IS NOT REMOVED
Two things a reader coming from another BSD may expect:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Sy The mail spool .
NextBSD ships no mail transport, so there is none to clean up.
.It
.Sy System V IPC objects .
Removing those has not been verified against this kernel, so
.Nm
does not pretend to.
An account that held semaphores or shared memory may leave them behind.
.El
.Pp
Files the user owned outside their home are also left alone.
Finding them means walking the filesystem, which on a large disk takes longer
than an operator expects and can remove something shared.
.Xr find 1
with
.Fl user
is the right tool when that is wanted.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Groups.plist" -compact
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Users.plist
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Groups.plist
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Domain.plist
present when this machine is the directory server, in which case
.Nm
manages the accounts here and never consults
.Pa /Network
.It Pa /Network/Library/DirectoryServices/Domain.plist
present when this machine is bound to a server, in which case
.Nm
refuses: the account belongs on the server
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Binding.plist
names that server for the message; it does not decide the role
.It Pa /Local/Users/ Ns Ar name
the home directory
.It Pa /var/cron/tabs/ Ns Ar name
.It Pa /var/at/jobs
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 4n -compact
.It 0
The account was removed.
.It 1
Usage error, or a batch file with a failing line.
.It 2
Refused.
Nothing was removed.
.It 3
Database error: a property list would not parse, the lock was held, or a write
failed.
.It 4
The confirmation was answered no.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Remove an account, keeping the files:
.Pp
.Dl # rmuser --keep-home joe
.Pp
Remove several without being asked:
.Pp
.Dl # rmuser -y joe kate sam
.Pp
Remove a list, saying what happens:
.Pp
.Dl # rmuser -v -f /tmp/leavers
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr find 1 ,
.Xr passwd 1 ,
.Xr adduser 8 ,
.Xr pw 8
.Sh HISTORY
This
.Nm
was written for NextBSD, to remove an account from the DirectoryServices
property lists.
It keeps the confirmation-first shape the command has always had, and drops the
cleanup steps that no longer apply to this system.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Joseph Maloney
